   "text": {
    "L1": "A circle is made of all the points that are the same distance from one fixed point, the centre.",
    "L2": "Some people think the radius changes in different directions. That is not true.",
    "L3": "The distance from the centre to the circle is the radius, and it is the same every way.",
    "L4": "For a diameter of 10 centimetres, the radius is half: 10 divided by 2 equals 5 centimetres.",
    "L5": "Open the compass to 5 centimetres with a ruler. The point stays on the centre while the pencil turns.",
    "L6": "Keep the compass opening fixed as you draw. Changing the opening would not make the intended circle.",
    "L7": "A diameter passes through the centre and is twice the radius. A line across the circle that misses the centre is a shorter chord.",
    "L8": "An arc is part of a circle. Now use the radius to choose the diameter."
   },
